## Putting it All Toghether: Revisiting the Final Project


In the *Tap for More* project, students design an interactive explore-style app that fetches live data from an external API and presents it in a grid of cards with a detail view.

Inspired by apps like Pinterest, Yelp, TikTok, and news feeds, students curate content and design how users explore and learn more about individual items.

<img src="https://images.code.org/b6518963d37d9630807dcd0085aa5036-image-1770400989649.png" alt="Sample explore-style app with grid of cards and detail view" width="750px" style="display:block; margin-left:auto; margin-right:auto; border-radius:10px;"><br>

<p style="font-size:14pt;">
  <a href="https://erikdillaman.github.io/U5_Project_Prototype_One/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Explore a sample student project</a>
</p>

---

## How the Unit Builds Toward This Project

Across the lessons, students progressively develop the skills they need to succeed:

<div style="max-width:760px; margin:24px auto;">

  <div style="width:520px; margin-left:174px; background:#f3f0ff; border:2px solid #6b7280; border-radius:10px; padding:18px 22px; margin-bottom:14px; font-size:18px;">
    <strong>4.</strong> Design defensively to keep apps usable when data is  <strong>delayed, incomplete, or unavailable</strong> 
  </div>

  <div style="width:520px; margin-left:116px; background:#f7f8fa; border:2px solid #6b7280; border-radius:10px; padding:18px 22px; margin-bottom:14px; font-size:18px;">
    <strong>3.</strong> Implement  <strong>error handling</strong>  to prevent app crashes
  </div>

  <div style="width:520px; margin-left:58px; background:#f2fcf7; border:2px solid #6b7280; border-radius:10px; padding:18px 22px; margin-bottom:14px; font-size:18px;">
    <strong>2.</strong> Parse and work with  <strong>JSON data structures</strong> 
  </div>

  <div style="width:520px; margin-left:0px; background:#f3f0ff; border:2px solid #6b7280; border-radius:10px; padding:18px 22px; margin-bottom:24px; font-size:18px;">
    <strong>1.</strong> Use <b>async/await</b> to manage asynchronous API calls 
  </div>

</div>


Each concept appears first in isolation, then comes together in the final project where students integrate data, logic, and user experience design.

